        <body>
            <div xml:id="d345">

                <pb facs="0792" n="696" sameAs="frus:frus1879/pg696"/>

                <head>No. 316.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Bingham to Mr. Evarts.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >United States Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Tokei,
                                Japan</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1879-10-22"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">October</hi> 22, 1879</date>. (Received
                        November 28.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 994.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to inclose herewith a copy
                    of a treaty recently negotiated by the Japanese Government with that of Corea
                    for the opening of an additional port in the latter country. You will observe
                    that by the terms of this treaty “from the month of March next the Corean
                    Government will open the port of Gensan, situated in Kan kion-do, to trade with
                    the Japanese, who shall be entitled to establish a settlement at
                    Chotoku-san.</p>
                <p>This new port is distant from the Corean capital only about forty miles.</p>
                <p>With the exception of China and Japan, Corea seems indisposed to hold treaty
                    relations with foreign powers.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>JOHN A. BINGHAM.</signed></closer>



                <xi:include href="d345-a1.xml" xpointer="d345-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
